  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "A^k = \\sum_{j=0}^{n-1} a_{k,j}\\, A^j,",
    "plain": "Cayley–Hamilton says every power of a matrix beyond degree $n-1$ collapses back into a combination of the first $n$ powers. So $A^k$ for any $k$ is determined by reducing $x^k$ modulo the characteristic polynomial — giving an explicit closed form for repeated matrix multiplication and for linear recurrences/transition matrices.",
    "whyMatters": []
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "M^2 = (tr M) . M - (det M) . I for any 2x2 matrix over a commutative ring (explicit 2x2 Cayley-Hamilton)",
      "M^(n+1) = u(n+1) . M - (det M . u n) . I, with u the Lucas sequence u0=0,u1=1,u(k+2)=t u(k+1) - d u(k)",
      "Reindexed form M^n = u(n) . M - (det M . u(n-1)) . I for n>0",
      "Fibonacci matrix showcase: trace 1, det -1, Lucas recursion = Fibonacci recurrence; M^2 = !![2,1;1,1]"
    ],
    "open": [],
    "goal": "Explicit closed form for powers of a 2x2 matrix from the characteristic polynomial (DONE)."
