    "insights": [
      "Only ONE-step induction is needed: multiplying M^(n+1)=u(n+1).M - d u(n).I by M makes an M^2 that 2x2 Cayley-Hamilton reduces immediately, so two-step induction is unnecessary.",
      "The scalar recursion that drops out IS the Lucas sequence of the first kind for X^2-tX+d; (1,-1) recovers Fibonacci, (2,-1) Pell.",
      "Holds over any commutative nontrivial ring — no eigenvalues/diagonalisability — because only Cayley-Hamilton + ring arithmetic is used.",
      "match_scalars splits the matrix identity into two scalar identities on the free module {M, I}, then ring closes after unfolding the Lucas recurrence (lucasU_add_two by rfl).",
      "Key Mathlib inputs: Matrix.charpoly_fin_two (needs [Nontrivial R]) and Matrix.aeval_self_charpoly; Algebra.algebraMap_eq_smul_one to evaluate C-coefficients."
    ],
    "mathlibGaps": [
      "Mathlib has the abstract Cayley-Hamilton (aeval_self_charpoly) and charpoly_fin_two but NOT the packaged 2x2 closed form M^2 = tr.M - det.I nor the Lucas-sequence power formula."
